WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.590
Interpretation begins with observation.

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:04.944
Describe the pattern's location, shape, size, apparent contrast, boundary,

00:00:04.944 --> 00:00:06.807
and relationship to nearby features.

00:00:06.807 --> 00:00:12.023
Avoid naming a cause before the visible evidence and operating context have been considered.

00:00:12.023 --> 00:00:17.612
Compare the feature with an appropriate background or similar component under similar load and conditions.

00:00:17.612 --> 00:00:21.338
A hot spot is meaningful only relative to expected behavior.

00:00:21.338 --> 00:00:23.573
Uniformly warm equipment may be normal,

00:00:23.573 --> 00:00:27.299
while a small difference among matching components may deserve review.

00:00:27.299 --> 00:00:32.143
False impressions can come from reflections, sun loading, retained heat, airflow, wet surfaces,

00:00:32.143 --> 00:00:35.124
insulation differences, angle, distance, or automatic display scaling.

00:00:35.124 --> 00:00:39.595
Moving the viewpoint or changing the comparison can help reveal a reflection

00:00:39.595 --> 00:00:41.458
without proving the final cause.

00:00:41.458 --> 00:00:43.321
Preserve the scale and settings.

00:00:43.321 --> 00:00:46.674
Palette changes can make a small difference look dramatic.

00:00:46.674 --> 00:00:51.145
Confirm whether the file contains radiometric data and whether measurement assumptions such

00:00:51.145 --> 00:00:53.753
as emissivity and reflected temperature are appropriate.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:55.616
Separate observation, judgment, and diagnosis.

00:00:55.616 --> 00:00:59.342
An observation may be an area of elevated apparent temperature.

00:00:59.342 --> 00:01:03.441
A judgment may be that it is anomalous compared with peers.

00:01:03.441 --> 00:01:07.539
A diagnosis of electrical failure or moisture intrusion requires domain evidence.

00:01:07.539 --> 00:01:09.775
Use confidence language and identify alternatives.

00:01:09.775 --> 00:01:13.873
Recommend authorized follow-up such as repeat collection, visible inspection, records review,

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:14.991
or specialist testing.

00:01:14.991 --> 00:01:20.580
Thermal imagery is strongest when it narrows questions rather than pretending to answer every one.
